@page "/homework"
@rendermode InteractiveServer

@* @rendermode InteractiveServer is what allows the checkboxes below to actually respond to clicks.
   Without it, this page still renders and looks completely normal — but nothing would react.
   Remember this line. It is the #1 reason a Blazor button appears to do nothing. *@

   @{
    #region Read This For More Info On RenderMode or skip all the way down until you see <PageTitle>Homework</PageTitle>
    @*  ============================================================================
    WHAT @rendermode INTERACTIVESERVER ACTUALLY DOES
    ============================================================================

    Short version: it is the line that makes buttons work. Delete it and this page
    still loads, still looks completely correct, and every click does nothing at all.
    No error, no warning, no red text in the console. Just silence.

    That silent failure is the single most common thing to trip people up in Blazor,
    so it is worth understanding rather than just copying the line around.

    ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    WITHOUT IT (this is the default)
    ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

    A page with no render mode uses "static server-side rendering". Here is the
    entire lifecycle:

        1. You request the page.
        2. The server runs your C#, builds the HTML, and sends it.
        3. The server forgets your component ever existed.
        4. You are now looking at a plain HTML document.

    Step 3 is the important one. Your @code block is not paused or waiting. It is
    gone. The currentCount variable, the bools below, all of it was thrown away
    the instant the HTML went out the door.

    So when you click a button, what is supposed to happen? There is no C# left
    running anywhere to handle it. The browser has flat HTML and nothing else.
    Blazor does not even bother attaching the click handler.

    This is also why nothing errors out. From the framework's point of view
    nothing went wrong. The page rendered exactly as asked. It just happens to
    be a photograph of a page rather than a working one.

    ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    WITH IT
    ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

    Adding @rendermode InteractiveServer changes step 3. Instead of discarding
    the component, the server keeps it alive in memory, and the browser opens a
    persistent connection back to the server (a WebSocket, managed by a library
    called SignalR). That live connection is called a "circuit".

    Now a click looks like this:

        1. You click a checkbox.
        2. The browser sends a tiny message up the connection: "checkbox 2, clicked".
        3. The server finds your still-alive component and runs the C#.
        4. The server works out precisely which bits of the page changed.
        5. It sends just those changes back down.
        6. JavaScript patches those spots in the DOM.

    Note what is NOT happening: the page never reloads and no full HTML is
    re-sent. Only the difference travels. That is why it feels instant.

    Note also where your C# is running: on the server, not in the browser. The
    browser is essentially a remote control. This is why you can talk to a
    database directly from a component later on without exposing anything.

    ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    WHY THE CHECKBOXES BELOW RESET WHEN YOU RELOAD
    ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

    Your circuit lives in the server's memory, and reloading the page throws the
    old one away and starts a fresh one. Fresh circuit, fresh component, fields
    back to their starting values.

    Blazor keeps state in memory, not on disk and not in the browser. If you want
    something to survive a refresh you have to deliberately put it somewhere that
    outlives the circuit, like a database. Nothing persists by accident.

    It is also why you might see an "Attempting to reconnect" banner if your wifi
    hiccups. The connection dropped, and with it the live component.

    ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    OTHER MODES YOU WILL SEE MENTIONED
    ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

    Ignore these for now, but so the names are not a mystery when you google:

        InteractiveServer        C# runs on the server, browser stays connected.
                                 (What we are using.)

        InteractiveWebAssembly   Your C# is downloaded and runs inside the browser.
                                 No live connection needed, but a slower first load
                                 and it cannot touch your database directly.

        InteractiveAuto          Uses Server for the first visit, then quietly
                                 switches to WebAssembly once it has downloaded.

    ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    WHERE THE LINE CAN GO
    ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

    Per page, like here, right under the @page line. Or globally for the whole
    app, by putting @rendermode="InteractiveServer" on the <Routes /> component
    in App.razor. Our project sets it per page, which is why you will see this
    line on Counter.razor too.

    ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    THE PRACTICAL TAKEAWAY
    ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

    When a button does nothing and there is no error message, check for this line
    first, before you start rewriting your event handler. Nine times out of ten
    that is the whole problem.

    Static rendering is not a broken mode, by the way. It is faster and lighter,
    and it is the right choice for pages that only display things. It only becomes
    a problem when you forget you are in it.
    ============================================================================ *@
       #endregion
   }

<PageTitle>Homework</PageTitle>

<div class="container py-4">

    <div class="mb-5">
        <h1 class="display-5 fw-bold text-primary">Homework</h1>
        <p class="lead text-secondary">
            Two parts. The first is styling only, the second adds a bit of C#.
            Take your time and break things — nothing here matters.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ PART 1 ============ -->
    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">
        <div class="card-header bg-primary text-white">
            <h4 class="mb-0">Part 1  Style the Home page</h4>
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <p>
                Open <code>Components/Pages/Home.razor</code> and make it look like something other than
                the default template. No C# for this part. It is pure HTML and Bootstrap classes.
            </p>

            <p class="fw-semibold mt-4">It has to include all of these:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>A heading that is <span class="text-danger">not</span> the default black</li>
                <li>At least three different text utility classes (size, weight, italics, alignment, color  your pick)</li>
                <li>One <code>card</code></li>
                <li>One <code>button</code> It does not have to do anything yet</li>
                <li>One <code>badge</code></li>
            </ul>

            <div class="alert alert-info mt-4 mb-0">
                <strong>Look the classes up rather than asking me.</strong>
                Getting comfortable in the Bootstrap docs is genuinely half of this assignment 
                you will be back there constantly for the rest of the project.
                <a href="https://getbootstrap.com/docs/5.3/" target="_blank" class="alert-link">getbootstrap.com/docs/5.3</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ PART 2 ============ -->
    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">
        <div class="card-header bg-success text-white">
            <h4 class="mb-0">Part 2  A greeting page</h4>
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <p>
                Make a <strong>new page</strong> with a textbox and a button. The user types their name,
                clicks the button, and a greeting appears on the page saying <em>Hello {their name}!</em>
            </p>

            <p class="fw-semibold mt-4">Starting it:</p>
            <ol>
                <li>Copy <code>Counter.razor</code> and rename the copy</li>
                <li>Change the <code>@@page</code> route at the top to something new</li>
                <li>Add a link to it in <code>NavMenu.razor</code></li>
                <li>Delete the counter bits and build your own thing</li>
            </ol>

            <p class="fw-semibold mt-4">Rules:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>The greeting shows up <strong>on the page</strong>, not in a popup</li>
                <li>Nothing should be visible before the button is clicked</li>
                <li>Keep the <code>@@rendermode InteractiveServer</code> line at the top you need it</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="alert alert-warning mt-4 mb-0">
                <strong>You will get stuck.</strong> That is the assignment working as intended.
                Sit with it a bit before messaging me  but do message me rather than
                burning an entire evening on one line.
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ STRETCH ============ -->
    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">
        <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white">
            <h4 class="mb-0">Stretch goals</h4>
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <p class="text-secondary">Optional, roughly easiest to hardest. Do none, do all.</p>
            <ul class="mb-0">
                <li>Disable the button while the textbox is empty</li>
                <li>Add a Clear button that resets everything</li>
                <li>Keep a running list of every name entered and show it underneath</li>
                <li>Pick a random greeting from a list, so it changes each click</li>
                <li>Make it an actual browser popup <span class="badge bg-danger">hard</span>  this one needs JavaScript interop, come find me</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SUBMITTING ============ -->
    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">
        <div class="card-header bg-secondary text-white">
            <h4 class="mb-0">Turning it in</h4>
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <p>Push it to your own branch. That part is not optional  the Git practice matters as much as the code.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Work on a branch named <code>yourname/homework</code></li>
                <li>At least <strong>three commits</strong>, with messages that say what you actually did</li>
                <li>Pushed, even if it is unfinished or broken</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mb-0 text-secondary fst-italic">
                "Fixed stuff" is not a commit message. "Added clear button to greeting page" is.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ PROGRESS ============ -->
    <div class="card shadow-sm">
        <div class="card-header">
            <h5 class="mb-0">Progress</h5>
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">

            @* Each checkbox uses @bind to tie itself to a bool field down in the @code block.
               @bind is two-way: ticking the box updates the variable, and changing the variable
               updates the box. You will want this exact concept for your textbox in Part 2 —
               it works the same way on an <input type="text">, just bound to a string instead. *@

            <div class="form-check">
                <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" id="c1" @bind="part1Done" />
                <label class="form-check-label" for="c1">Part 1  Home page styled</label>
            </div>

            <div class="form-check">
                <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" id="c2" @bind="part2Done" />
                <label class="form-check-label" for="c2">Part 2  Greeting page works</label>
            </div>

            <div class="form-check">
                <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" id="c3" @bind="pushedDone" />
                <label class="form-check-label" for="c3">Pushed to my branch</label>
            </div>

            @* This block only renders when all three bools are true.
               An @if in Razor is just a normal C# if — it decides whether the HTML inside it
               gets sent to the browser at all. Same trick you need for hiding your greeting
               until the button is clicked. *@

            @if (part1Done && part2Done && pushedDone)
            {
                <div class="alert alert-success mt-3 mb-0">
                    Done. Nice work  go break something else.
                </div>
            }

            <p class="text-muted small mt-3 mb-0">
                These reset when you reload. Ask yourself why, and you will understand
                most of what you need to know about how Blazor holds state.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

</div>

@code {
    // Three bools backing the checkboxes above. A bool is either true or false, nothing else.
    // They start as false, which is why every box is unticked when the page first loads.
    private bool part1Done = false;
    private bool part2Done = false;
    private bool pushedDone = false;

    // Notice there is no method down here at all — no button click to handle.
    // @bind wires the checkboxes straight to these fields on its own, and Blazor
    // re-renders the page whenever one of them changes. That re-render is what makes
    // the success message appear the moment the third box gets ticked.
}
